extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Styling | Colours"
	- pagedescription = "Colours in Tablechart: the text colour, one colour for all bars, colours for a waterfall, and a palette."

block content
	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Colours
				p.lead The text colour, one colour for all bars, colours for a waterfall, and a palette.

	+section("The text colour")
		p A chart has the text colour of the element it is in. The bars, the lines, the names and the numbers next to the bars all get that colour. Without a palette, each next line or slice is a lighter shade of it. If the page has another text colour in dark mode, the chart gets that colour too. See #[a(href=url('/styling/light-dark.html')) Light and dark].

		p In this example, no colour is set, so the chart has the text colour of this page:

		.plain
			+chart
				figure(data-chart="column")
					table
						tbody
							each row in [['Spring', '58'], ['Summer', '72'], ['Autumn', '70'], ['Winter', '91']]
								tr
									th= row[0]
									td= row[1]

		p If you want another colour for the chart only, set #[code --tablechart-color] on the figure. If you want the bars in another colour than the text, see #[a(href="#one-colour") One colour].

	+section("One colour")
		p #[code --tablechart-bar-color] sets the colour of all bars, and of a single line.

		+style.
			.orange {
				--tablechart-bar-color: #e4572e;
			}

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure.orange(data-chart="column")
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Spring', '58'], ['Summer', '72'], ['Autumn', '70'], ['Winter', '91']]
							tr(data-kind=row[2])
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

	+section("Colours with low contrast")
		p If a column or bar has a colour with less than 3:1 contrast with the background, it gets an edge that has enough (WCAG 1.4.11). The edge is in the colour of the bar, moved towards the text colour as far as needed. It is inside the bar, so the bar keeps its size. Where the bar is on the baseline, the baseline is its edge. Then the baseline gets a contrast of 4.5:1, so it is darker than the edges. #[code --tablechart-bar-edge-width] sets the width of the edge (1px by default). If you want no edge, set #[code --tablechart-bar-edge: none].

		+style.
			.pale {
				--tablechart-bar-color: light-dark(#f3d77a, #4a4a8a);
			}

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure.pale(data-chart="column")
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Spring', '58'], ['Summer', '72'], ['Autumn', '-30'], ['Winter', '91']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure.pale(data-chart="bar" style="--tablechart-bar-radius: 6px")
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Spring', '58'], ['Summer', '72'], ['Autumn', '70'], ['Winter', '91']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

		p A palette works the same way: each series gets an edge only if its own colour has too little contrast. In the chart below, the first two colours get an edge and the third does not. Between the parts of a stack is a gap in the background colour. The parts have no edge along it, and the edges at the sides go on across it. The squares in the legend and in the tooltip get the same edge.

		+style.
			.pastel {
				--tablechart-color-1: light-dark(#a8d8ea, #2e4a6b);
				--tablechart-color-2: light-dark(#f6c6a8, #6b3a2e);
				--tablechart-color-3: light-dark(#5a7d9a, #9fc3e0);
			}

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure.pastel(data-chart="column" data-chart-stack)
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Season
							th Apples
							th Pears
							th Plums
					tbody
						each row in [['Spring', '20', '14', '9'], ['Summer', '31', '22', '15'], ['Autumn', '42', '28', '12'], ['Winter', '18', '11', '6']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]
								td= row[3]

		p A slice of a donut or pie gets the same edge, all round its shape: along the outside and the inside of the ring, along the gaps, and round the corners if the slices are rounded. A pie has no inside, so it has no edge there. The dots in the legend get the edge too.

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure.pastel(data-chart="donut")
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Fruit
							th Share
					tbody
						each row in [['Apples', '45'], ['Pears', '30'], ['Plums', '25']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure.pastel(data-chart="donut" style="--tablechart-slice-radius: 8px")
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Fruit
							th Share
					tbody
						each row in [['Apples', '45'], ['Pears', '30'], ['Plums', '25']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

		p In a combo chart, the columns get the edge. A line has no edge, so in the chart below it has a dark colour of its own.

		+style.
			.pale-combo {
				--tablechart-color-1: light-dark(#f3d77a, #4a4a8a);
				--tablechart-color-2: light-dark(#7a4f00, #e8d48a);
			}

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure.pale-combo(data-chart="column")
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Season
							th Sold
							th(data-chart="line") Target
					tbody
						each row in [['Spring', '58', '50'], ['Summer', '72', '80'], ['Autumn', '30', '45'], ['Winter', '91', '85']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]

	+section("Waterfall colours")
		p These set the colours of rising steps, falling steps and totals. The number inside a bar gets a colour with enough contrast with the bar. See #[a(href=url('/styling/text.html#numbers-inside-bars-or-columns')) Numbers inside bars or columns].

		+style.
			.warm {
				--tablechart-up-color: light-dark(#3f7a3f, #44803f);
				--tablechart-down-color: #8a5a1c;
				--tablechart-total-color: #6b6256;
			}

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure.warm(data-chart="waterfall")
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Picked', '74.5', 'total'], ['Windfalls', '28.2'], ['Bruised', '-16.1'], ['Peelings', '-13.3'], ['Pressed', '73.3', 'total']]
							tr(data-kind=row[2])
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

	+section("A palette")
		p Donut slices use #[code --tablechart-color-1], #[code --tablechart-color-2] and on, in order. If a colour is not set, that slice uses the default.

		+style.
			.fruity {
				--tablechart-color-1: #b4552d;
				--tablechart-color-2: #b47a2f;
				--tablechart-color-3: #678f67;
				--tablechart-color-4: #8f8576;
			}

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure.fruity(data-chart="donut")
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Picked', '74.5', 'total'], ['Juiced', '38.2'], ['Eaten fresh', '18.1'], ['Sold whole', '12.4'], ['Composted', '5.8']]
							tr(data-kind=row[2])
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

		p If a line chart has several lines, the lines use the palette too.

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure.fruity(data-chart="line")
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Year
							th North
							th South
							th East
					tbody
						each row in [['2016', '41', '30', '22'], ['2019', '58', '39', '35'], ['2022', '77', '52', '41'], ['2025', '105', '61', '70']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]
								td= row[3]

	+section("Without a palette")
		p Without a palette, each slice or line is a bit lighter than the one before it. These variables set how much.

		table
			thead
				tr
					th Variable
					th Sets
			tbody
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-slice-color
					td The colour of the first slice. The text colour by default.
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-slice-fade
					td How much lighter each slice is: 15% by default.
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-slice-min
					td The lightest a slice gets: 20% of its colour by default.
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-series-color
					td The colour of the first line. The bar colour by default.
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-series-fade
					td How much lighter each line is: 30% by default. With more than three lines, the steps are smaller, so the last line is not lighter than #[code --tablechart-series-min].
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-series-min
					td The lightest a line gets: 40% of its colour by default.
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-series-muted-share
					td The strength of a line next to a highlighted line: 45% by default.
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-series-own-color
					td The colour of one line, set on [data-series="2"] and so on.

	+section("A range of colours")
		p If you set an end colour, the lines, the parts of a stack or the slices go from the first colour to the end colour, in even steps, instead of getting lighter. With two lines they are the first and the end colour; with three there is also the colour exactly in between, and so on.
		p If both colours have a contrast of at least 3:1 with the background, and both are darker than the background, or both lighter, every colour in between has that too. If both have at least 4.5:1, the numbers inside the parts can stay in the background colour.

		+style.
			.dark-range {
				--tablechart-bar-color: light-dark(#0b5d66, #236d76);
				--tablechart-series-color-end: light-dark(#1f3a8a, #4060b4);
			}

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure.dark-range(data-chart="column" data-chart-stack)
				figcaption
					b Apples sold
					br
					span thousands
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Year
							th Farm shop
							th Market
							th Online
					tbody
						each row in [['2023', '64', '38', '22'], ['2024', '71', '45', '30'], ['2025', '69', '57', '26'], ['2026', '80', '62', '41']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]
								td= row[3]

		table
			thead
				tr
					th Variable
					th Sets
			tbody
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-series-color-end
					td The colour of the last line or part. The first is #[code --tablechart-series-color], or the bar colour. #[code --tablechart-series-color-start] is another name for the first colour, to go with the end colour.
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-slice-color-end
					td The colour of the last slice. The first is #[code --tablechart-slice-color]. #[code --tablechart-slice-color-start] is another name for it, to go with the end colour.
